/-!
# Matrix representations of frame and lifted-graphic matroids

Funk, Pivotto and Slilaty, *Matrix representations of frame and lifted-graphic matroids correspond
to gain functions* (arXiv:1609.05574v5). The modules follow the sections of the manuscript.

`Section1_Projective` gives the row-space form of projective equivalence, `Section1_Elementary` the
source's elementary operations, and `Section1_Prop11` proves that the two agree and Proposition 1.1.
`Section1_Canonical` defines oriented graphs and the canonical frame and lift matrices.
`Section1_Conjectures` proves that Conjectures 1 and 2 fail as literally stated because of joints.
`Section2_Gains` defines switching and scaling of gain functions, `Section2_Walks` walks and paths,
and `Section2_Cycles` cycles and thetas. `Section2_Balance` gives the gain of a walk and proves that
balance of a cycle does not depend on the walk, `Section2_Biased` defines biased graphs and `B_γ`,
and `Section2_BiasedExamples` applies them to three parallel links. `Section2_Forests` proves
Proposition 2.1 and `Section2_Normalized` Proposition 2.2. `Section2_Minors` defines deletion,
contraction and induced gain functions and proves Proposition 2.3. `Section2_Connectivity` defines
separations and `k`-connected graphs and extends forests to maximal ones. `Section2_Classes`
defines balanced, almost-balanced, properly unbalanced and tangled biased graphs and joints.
`Section2_Matroids` defines frame and lift circuits, matroid connectivity and uniform matroids.
`Section2_LinkMinors` defines link minors, their induced gains and the two contractions of a joint.
`Section2_InducedRealization` lifts cycles of a link minor, and `Section2_InducedMinors` and
`Section2_JointContractions` prove Proposition 2.4. `Section2_MatrixMinors` defines `A / e` and
proves Lemma 2.5 (i), `Section2_MatrixContractions` proves it for links and refutes the printed
frame case, `Section2_MatrixJoints` treats joints, and `Section2_MatrixEquivalence` proves that
projective equivalence passes to minors of a matrix.
`Section2_DeltaY` defines the Δ-Y and Y-Δ exchanges of biased graphs, and `Section2_DeltaYExamples`
shows that the printed `∇_Y B` can contain a non-cycle.
`Section2_CycleDegrees` proves that a vertex of a loopless graph meets a cycle in zero or two edges
and lists the seven cycles of `K₄`. `Section2_FiniteMatroids` builds finite matroids from their
independent sets and decides their flats. `Section2_MatroidExchange` defines `M(K₄)` and the Δ-Y and
Y-Δ exchanges of matroids, `Section2_ExchangeGraphs` and `Section2_ExchangeMatroids` compute them on
small graphs, and `Section2_DeltaYMatroids` states Proposition 2.8 and shows that part (ii) needs
`Y` to be a triad. `Section2_Representable` defines the matrix representations of a matroid and
their projective classes, and `Section2_RepresentationExchange` refutes Proposition 2.9 as printed
and states it for a coindependent triangle. `Section2_ExchangeMatrices` defines `I(K₄)` and the
exchanges of matrices and proves Proposition 2.11 for identity gains on the exchanged edges.
`Section2_GainExchange` refutes Proposition 2.10 (i) as printed and proves (i) and (ii) for gains
that are the identity on the triangle. `Section2_ExchangeForms` proves that projectively equivalent
matrices represent the same matroid and brings a represented triangle to the last three columns of
`I(K₄)`. `Section2_TriadForms` gives the row vector of a cocircuit, `Section2_TriadColumns` refutes
the printed claim that every triad takes the first three columns of `I(K₄)`, and `Section2_K4Forms`
proves it for an independent triad. `Section2_K4Matrix` proves that every representation of `M(K₄)`
is projectively equivalent to `I(K₄)`. `Section2_MatrixMatroids` builds the matroid of a matrix and
reads its flats off spans, and `Section2_K4Flats` gives the flats of `M(K₄)` by its triangles.
`Section2_TriangleConnection` adjoins `I(K₄)` to a matrix across a triangle,
`Section2_ConnectionFlats` and `Section2_ConnectionMatroid` prove that its matroid is the
generalized parallel connection. `Section2_DualFlats` reads the flats and independent sets of the
dual of a represented matroid off spans. `Section2_K4NewColumns` gives the edges of `K₄` on the
rows of a split vertex, `Section2_CoconnectionMatrix` splits the vertex of a triad into a
triangle, `Section2_CoconnectionMaps` relates the columns of the two matrices, and
`Section2_CoconnectionFlats` and `Section2_CoconnectionDeletion` prove that the dual of its matroid
is the generalized parallel connection of `M(A)*` and `M(K₄)`, and that deleting the triad leaves
`M(∇_Y A)*`. `Section2_ExchangedMatrices` proves that `Δ_X A` represents `Δ_X M(A)` and `∇_Y A`
represents `∇_Y M(A)`, and `Section2_NablaZeroRow` proves that the latter fails as printed, without
the zero row of `IsTriadForm`. `Section2_BiasedMatroids` states what the source cites
from Zaslavsky on `F(G, B)`, `L(G, B)`, their canonical matrices and minors, and proves that the
biased graph determines them and that they agree without vertex-disjoint unbalanced cycles.
`Section2_FullRankLift` deletes one row per component of a canonical lift matrix, and shows that the
rows left are independent with an unbalanced cycle and need not be without one.
`Section2_UnbalancingClasses` defines the unbalancing classes at a balancing vertex of an
almost-balanced biased graph. `Section2_PathSurgery` cuts and joins paths, `Section2_CyclePaths`
splits a cycle through two edges at a vertex into these edges and a path, `Section2_Rerouting`
reroutes a cycle through a theta, and `Section2_RerouteBias` proves that if every cycle missing a
vertex is balanced, the cycles through two edges at it have one bias. `Section2_LinkTheta` puts
three links at a vertex on a theta, and `Section2_PairCircuits` sorts two edges at a vertex with a
path between their other ends into frame circuits and independent sets.
`Section2_UnbalancingRelation` proves what §2.5 claims of the unbalancing classes: their cycles
have one bias, `∼` is transitive, and each pair of edges gives circuits or independent sets.
`Section2_Unrolling` defines the unrolling `(Ĝ, B̂)`, `Section2_UnrollingFailures` refutes its
printed `B̂`, and `Section2_UnrollingClasses` refutes the printed claim on its unbalancing classes.
`Section2_UnrollingEmptyJ` proves that with `J` empty a rollable biased graph is its own unrolling,
as the source sets it, and `Section2_UnrollingSameClasses` that the unrolling keeps the unbalancing
classes when the joints off the vertex are connected. `Section2_UnrollingLinks` relates the cycles
of `B̂` to `∼`, and `Section2_UnrollingTheta` proves that the unrolling is a biased graph.
`Section2_RollUps` defines the roll-ups of an unrolling and counts them, and `Section2_RollUpClaims`
proves what §2.5 claims of them. `Section2_RollUpClasses` proves that each roll-up keeps the
unbalancing classes of `(Ĝ, B̂)`. `Section2_WalkMaps` carries walks along a map of the vertices,
`Section2_LoopUnrolling` defines the unrolling of loops to one vertex, and `Section2_LoopCircuits`
proves that its frame circuits are the cycles of the new graph and states what §2.5 claims of a
biased graph balanced off its joints.
`Section2_AgreeingGraphs` compares graphs that give a set of edges the same ends,
`Section2_CycleEars` and `Section2_TightHandcuffs` build cycles, thetas and tight handcuffs from
ears, and `Section2_RollUpCycles` describes the cycles, paths and handcuffs of a roll-up.
`Section2_RollUpClass` collects in `IsRollUpClass` what comparing the circuits of a roll-up uses,
`Section2_RollUpJoints` and `Section2_RollUpForward` prove that a frame circuit of a roll-up is one
of the biased graph rolled up, `Section2_RollUpThetas` and `Section2_RollUpBackward` the converse,
and `Section2_RollUpCircuits` deduces `F(G_U, B_U) = F(Ĝ, B̂) = F(G, B)`.
`Section2_ExchangePairs`, `Section2_ExchangeCycles`, `Section2_ExchangeLabels`,
`Section2_ExchangeBalance`, `Section2_ExchangeSwitching` and `Section2_ExchangeEquivalence` match
the cycles, balanced cycles and switching classes on both sides of a Δ-Y exchange (`IsExchange`),
and `Section2_ExchangeRealizes` and `Section2_ExchangeClasses` prove Proposition 2.10 (i) and (ii).
`Section2_ExchangeWalks`, `Section2_ExchangeTransfer` and `Section2_TransferCycles` carry walks,
paths and cycles across the exchange, `Section2_BalancedTripods` and `Section2_StarThetas` treat
thetas through the new vertex, and `Section2_DeltaBiased` deduces Proposition 2.6.
`Section2_NablaCycles` reads membership in `∇_Y B` off `B`, `Section2_NablaPaths` and
`Section2_NablaDetours` carry paths of `∇_Y G` through the centre of the star,
`Section2_NablaTripods`, `Section2_NablaCorners` and `Section2_NablaCovers` match the thetas of
`∇_Y G` with thetas of `G`, and `Section2_NablaBiased` deduces Proposition 2.7.
`Section2_NablaExamples` and `Section2_NablaThetaExamples` meet their hypotheses.
`Section2_Components` counts the components of a set of edges and `Section2_Nullity` its nullity
`|S| + c(S) - |V|`, which vanishes exactly on forests; by `Section2_CycleNullity` it is at least two
exactly when the set contains two distinct cycles. `Section2_CycleBicycles` finds a tight handcuff,
two vertex-disjoint cycles or a theta in any two distinct cycles, `Section2_BicycleNullity` bounds
the nullity of these and of loose handcuffs by two, and `Section2_BalancedSpan` proves that the
cycles spanned by a forest are balanced when its fundamental cycles are. `Section2_LiftIndep` and
`Section2_LiftMatroid` deduce that the lift circuits are the circuits of a matroid.
`Section2_FrameIndep` characterizes the sets containing no frame circuit as those with no balanced
cycle and no two linked cycles, `Section2_FrameCircuits` shows that no frame circuit lies in
another, `Section2_SpanningForests` counts the edges of pseudoforests, and `Section2_FrameAugment`
proves the exchange axiom; `Section2_FrameMatroid` deduces that the frame circuits are the circuits
of a matroid.
`Section2_DeletionCycles` identifies the cycles and forests of a deletion with those of the graph
avoiding the deleted edges, and `Section2_LiftContraction` deduces that the lift-type contraction of
a joint `e` gives the cycle matroid of `G \ e`.
`Section2_FrameContractGraph` describes the cycles and reachability of the frame-type contraction
of a joint, `Section2_FrameContractIndep` frame independence next to a joint and in a deletion, and
`Section2_FrameContractCore` frame independence after the contraction; `Section2_FrameContraction`
deduces that the frame-type contraction of a joint `e` agrees with `F(G, B) / e`.
`Section4_Switching` proves Proposition 4.1 and `Section4_Diagonal` proves Lemma 4.2.
`Section4_Rows` names what deleting a vertex leaves of a graph, `Section4_Graphs` defines `2C₃`,
`K₄` and the tube, and `Section4_Relations` and `Section4_LiftRows` read a projective equivalence
off the rows of `T`. `Section4_Small` deduces Lemmas 4.3 to 4.10, and `Section4_SmallExamples` meets
their hypotheses. `Section3_Classes` reads off cycles of two and three edges, `Section3_SmallGraphs`
decides which biased `2C₃`, tubes and `K₄` are properly unbalanced, and `Section3_SmallExamples`
gives concrete ones. `Section5_ProperlyUnbalanced` proves Theorem 5.1 from the rows of `T`.
`Section1_ThreeVertices` counts the edges of circuits on three vertices, `Section1_Fan` realizes
`U_{3,5}` as a frame matroid on three vertices, and `Section1_Theorem1` proves from it that
Theorem 1 (i) fails as printed on an almost-balanced biased graph. `Section4_TwoVertices` treats
the columns of loops and the cycles on two vertices, and `Section4_U24` proves Lemmas 4.11 and 4.12.
`Section4_TwoVertexCircuits` counts the edges of circuits on two vertices, and `Section4_U24Matroid`
proves that `𝖴₂` and `𝖴₃` represent `U_{2,4}`.
-/
